EXT. DRY WELL - HOMESTEAD - DAY

Flat, cracked earth to the horizon. A single stone well, rope rotted and trailing in the dirt. A windmill turns without conviction.

AMOS "OLD AMOS" TREVELYAN, 60s, sits on the well's lip, a canteen across his knees, bootless, feet in the dust.

CASSIUS DELANEY, 30s, rides up on a gray mare. Hat low, duster pale with road. He swings down and ties the horse to the windmill.

CASSIUS
Afternoon, Amos.

AMOS
Cassius. You're a long way from town.

CASSIUS
So are you.

AMOS
I live here.

CASSIUS
Not for long, I hear.

Amos unscrews the canteen. Tilts it. A few drops fall on his tongue.

AMOS
They send you to talk me out?

CASSIUS
Nobody sends me.

AMOS
That's what I thought.

CASSIUS
The bank wants the land.

AMOS
The bank can have the dust.

CASSIUS
It's the dust they're paying for.

AMOS
Then the bank's a fool.

Cassius crouches. Picks up a clod and crumbles it. He looks into the well.

CASSIUS
How deep was she?

AMOS
Forty feet when I dug her. Now? Maybe thirty-nine.

CASSIUS
You've been down there?

AMOS
Every morning.

CASSIUS
Why?

AMOS
To see if she changed her mind.

A long silence. The windmill creaks. A hawk crosses the sky.

CASSIUS
Amos, I've known you since I was a boy.

AMOS
You were a boy who stole my peaches.

CASSIUS
I was a boy who was hungry.

AMOS
Same thing.

Cassius laughs, short. Stands. Brushes his hands on his pants.

CASSIUS
I came to tell you something.

AMOS
Go on.

CASSIUS
There's water, Amos. East of here. A man in the hills found a spring.

Amos lowers the canteen. He turns his head slowly.

AMOS
How far?

CASSIUS
Two days. Maybe three, with a wagon.

AMOS
And who owns it?

CASSIUS
Nobody yet.

AMOS
(softly)
Nobody yet.

He looks into the well. Then, deliberately, he lifts one boot from the dust and pulls it on.

AMOS (CONT'D)
Cassius, what do you want for it?

CASSIUS
I want my name on the deed beside yours.

Amos stares at him for a long moment. Then he reaches behind the well, pulls out a shotgun, and levels it at Cassius's chest.

AMOS
That's what I thought.

CASSIUS
Amos.

AMOS
You didn't come to tell me. You came to see if I'd say yes.

CASSIUS
Put it down, old man.

AMOS
Where's the spring, Cassius?

CASSIUS
There is no spring.

Amos's finger tightens. Cassius does not move.

CASSIUS (CONT'D)
But there was a time you'd have believed me.

The windmill creaks. Amos lowers the gun, slowly. He sits back on the well, looking older. Cassius mounts his mare and rides off, and Amos does not watch him go.
